"Surah at-Takwir was revealed in Mecca and consists of 29 verses, 139 words, and 533 letters. The end-rhyme (fasila) of the surah consists of letters. According to what Imam Ahmad, at-Tirmidhi, and al-Hakim narrated from Ibn Umar, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Whoever wishes to look upon the Day of Resurrection as if seeing it with their own eyes, let them recite Surahs at-Takwir, al-Infitar, and al-Inshiqaq.' In the opening verses of the surah, twelve events that will occur on the Day of Resurrection are mentioned, and it is said, 'Every soul shall know what it has brought.' These events are: the folding up of the sun, the clouding of the stars, the setting of the mountains in motion, the abandonment of prized possessions, the gathering of wild beasts, the setting ablaze of the seas, the pairing of souls, the questioning of the girl buried alive, the opening of the records of deeds, the stripping away of the sky, the kindling of Hellfire, and the bringing near of Paradise. Some of these will take place in this world, and some in the Hereafter.\n\nThere are different views regarding the folding up of the sun (takwir al-shams). Takwir means folding, wrapping, and gathering together, as well as casting down, throwing away, and blinding. According to a narration from Ibn Abbas, the sun is joined to the Throne; it has been interpreted from Mujahid, Qatadah, and Hasan as the departure of its light; from Rabi' b. Khuthaym as its being cast away; and from Abu Salih as its being turned upside down. Al-Qurtubi states that the sun will be wound up like a turban and, after its light is removed, it will be cast away. The clouding/dimming of the stars (inkidar) has been explained in two senses: the extinguishing of their light or their falling down. Al-Bukhari narrated the view of their falling, and commentators have mostly preferred this. As for the setting of the mountains in motion, it is described as the shaking of the earth, the mountains being carded like wool, and their being hurled into the sky.\n\nIn the expression 'the abandonment of prized possessions' (ta'til al-'ishar), 'ishar' is the name given to ten-month pregnant camels and represents the most valuable property. In the face of the terror of the Day of Resurrection, their owners leave these possessions unattended. Some scholars have interpreted this expression as the drying up of clouds or as lands from which the tithe ('ushr) is collected. The gathering of wild beasts has been interpreted in three ways: their coming together out of fear, their perishing through death, or their being resurrected for retribution (qisas) and gathered at the place of assembly (mahshar). In the hadith, it is reported that the hornless sheep will claim its right from the horned one. The setting ablaze of the seas (tasjir) has been interpreted as the receding of waters through volcanoes, their being filled with fire, or waters overflowing and invading the earth. Some narrations state that the first six events take place in this world, and the last six in the Hereafter.\n\nThe pairing of souls (tazwij al-nufus) has been understood primarily in two senses. According to the first view, the good are brought together with the good, and the wicked with the wicked, and classified. This is supported by verses in Surahs as-Saffat and al-Waqi'ah. According to the second view, souls are restored to their bodies and reunited. The first view is considered stronger and denotes the classification at the place of assembly. The questioning of the girl buried alive (al-maw'udah) deals with the custom during the pre-Islamic period of ignorance (Jahiliyyah) of burying female children for various reasons. In the verse, this girl will be asked for what sin she was killed. This custom was practiced due to beliefs such as poverty, fear of shame, or regarding angels as the daughters of Allah.\n\nIslam took a severe stance against the custom of burying female children alive. In Surahs al-An'am and al-Isra, killing children out of fear of poverty was forbidden. The Prophet (peace be upon him) commanded Qays b. Asim, who had committed this act, to free a slave or sacrifice a camel for each girl buried. Scholars have stated that this command indicates recommendation (mandub), not obligation (wujub). Furthermore, it is narrated that regarding coitus interruptus ('azl, discharging semen outside during intercourse), the Prophet (peace be upon him) stated that it was 'concealed infanticide.' In jurisprudence (fiqh), 'azl is considered disliked (makruh). These verses and hadiths indicate that abortion and deliberately induced miscarriage are likewise major sins.\n\nOne of the scenes of the Day of Resurrection is asking the girl buried alive in the earth for what crime she was killed. Rather than interrogating the killer directly, directing this question to the oppressed victim carries the meaning of a severe rebuke and silencing (tabkit) in order to torment the killer's conscience and make him confess his wrongdoing. Here, bringing forward the innocent little girl as the plaintiff is an effective warning that no defense will be valid on that day.\n\nThe opening of the records of deeds has been interpreted in two ways: First, that the deceased person's record is rolled up and then opened on the Day of Resurrection for the reckoning. Second, that after the reckoning, the pages announcing the results are distributed, which is called 'the flying of the pages.' While the believer's page falls into their hand declaring 'in an exalted garden,' the disbeliever's falls declaring 'in Hellfire.' The first meaning is more suitable here; the verses in the subsequent surah also explain this.\n\nThe stripping away of the sky (kasht) is the removal of the covering of the sky, just like skinning an animal. Rather than annihilation, this signifies the full manifestation of realities in the new realm. Indeed, al-Nisaburi states that when the sky is opened and removed, Paradise and the Throne above it will appear. On that day, the Throne of the Truth (al-Haqq) becomes manifest, and human souls remain between the manifestations of divine wrath and mercy.\n\nThe kindling of Hellfire, its being stoked ablaze for the transgressors, and the bringing near of Paradise to the righteous take place during the phase of final judgment. When these twelve events (the folding up of the sun, the clouding of the stars, the setting of the mountains in motion, etc.) occur, every soul will know what it has prepared. Every deed, good or evil, will be found in the record of deeds and placed on the scale, and the person will see the recompense for what they did. This knowledge occurs after the opening of the records and the stripping away of the sky.\n\nSome commentators have stated that lessons may be drawn from these verses for the minor resurrection (death) as well as the major Resurrection. For example, the folding up of the sun has been interpreted as the soul's separation from the body, and the falling of the stars as the loss of bodily faculties. However, the primary meaning belongs to the major Resurrection. Such interpretations do not harm faith; rather, they enhance the lesson. In the Quran, there are exoteric (zahir) and esoteric (batin) meanings; while believing in the manifest meaning is essential, it is also permissible to benefit from allusions and interpretations (ta'wil) that do not contradict it. It is necessary to avoid excess (ifrat) and negligence (tafrit) and find the middle path.\n\nThere are different narrations regarding the interpretation of the expression \"al-khunnas al-kawnas\" mentioned in these verses. According to what is transmitted from al-Hasan al-Basri, Qatadah, and Ali, this expression refers to stars in general. However, it is also narrated from Ali that it was interpreted specifically as the five planets. The word \"kunnas\" has been associated with \"kunus,\" which indicates the places where wild cattle and gazelles conceal themselves. For this reason, it is narrated from Ibn Abbas, Sa'id b. Jubayr, and al-Dahhak that it means gazelles, and from Ibn Mas'ud that it means wild cattle.\n\nAfter transmitting the narrations, Ibn Jarir stated that it is more appropriate to interpret this expression generally. According to him, Allah swore by things that sometimes disappear, sometimes course along, and return to their places. In Arabic, the word \"kunnas\" refers to the places where wild cattle and deer enter. It is also possible that it is used for the positions of the stars by way of metaphor (isti'arah). It would be more accurate to say that everything bearing these attributes is intended in the verse. In this case, a general metaphor is involved, and this metaphor is supported by the fact that the disappearing and coursing of the stars conforms to the flow of the verses.\n\nIn verses 17 and 18, oaths are sworn by the night and the morning. The word \"As'ase\" means both to come and to depart. The majority preferred the moment of the night's departure, which is the harbinger of morning. The breathing of the morning points to the pleasantness of the morning breeze. These oaths promise the Prophet (peace be upon him) and the Muslims that the Hereafter is better than this world. For them, this world is like a night turning toward morning, and the Resurrection is like an approaching morning.\n\nIn verses 19–25, it is emphasized that the Quran was brought by a noble messenger. This messenger is Gabriel (peace be upon him). He is an angel who is esteemed, powerful, trustworthy, and obeyed in the presence of the Lord of the Throne. It is stated that the Prophet (peace be upon him) is not mad; rather, he saw him on the clear horizon. It is narrated that Gabriel was seen in his original form twice. The Prophet is not withholding of the unseen; he receives the revelation with complete trust and conveys it. The Quran is not the word of outcast devils.\n\nIn verses 26–29, people are addressed: \"Where then are you going?\" The Quran is a reminder for all the worlds, especially for those who wish to be on the straight path. However, a person's willing to be on the straight path is contingent upon Allah's will. You cannot will unless Allah wills. This shows that human will is subordinate to Allah's will. One must not attribute success to oneself, but view it as Allah's favor. The verses contain both a warning and a consolation.\n\nThe expression \"you cannot will unless Allah wills\" in the final verses of Surah at-Takwir, though appearing absolute at first glance, actually contains a hidden object (maf'ul). The fact that the verb is transitive (muta'addi) indicates the presence of this object. From the context of the previous verse, \"for whoever among you wills to be on the straight path,\" it is understood that this object is \"to will to be on the straight path.\" That is, the meaning is: \"Unless Allah wills that you will to be on the straight path, you cannot will it.\" Verifying scholars have preferred this explanation.\n\nNevertheless, some have regarded the verb as intransitive (lazim) to preserve the generality of the address, understanding it as \"you cannot will anything.\" In this case, it is explicitly expressed that without Allah's willing, the servant can have no willing whatsoever, which is the precise view of the Ahl al-Sunnah. However, the specific restriction regarding being on the straight path is understood not through explicit expression, but through implication. The first explanation is more direct and accords better with the flow of speech.\n\nThis verse does not support a determinist view (jabr) that completely negates the will of servants. By virtue of the rule of \"istithna-i mufarragh\" (vacuous exception), the phrase \"unless Allah wills\" does not entirely eliminate the servant's willing; rather, it indicates that without Allah's willing, no willing can take place. The servant can will by Allah's will, and can even possess a willing that requires what is desired to come to pass. This is called \"cebr-i mutavassit\" (intermediate determinism). The Maturidi school states that although the servant's universal will (iradah kulliyyah, the power of will) is created, their particular will (iradah juz'iyyah) is an evaluated, subjective matter (amr i'tibari).\n\nIn a narration transmitted regarding the occasion of revelation of the verse, when Abu Jahl heard the verse \"for whoever among you wills to be on the straight path,\" he said, \"So the matter has been left to us; if we wish, we will be on the right path, and if we do not wish, we will not.\" Thereupon, this verse was revealed and showed that there is no absolute autonomy. The verse encourages not fatalistic compulsion, but asking Allah for guidance to the straight path. The warning and the necessity of willing are understood through implicit indication.\n\nPeople must seek the truth by willing to be on the straight path and benefit from this reminder. However, those who attain success must recognize it as coming from Allah and not forget that He has the power to remove and extinguish that willing. By the statement \"for whoever among you wills to be on the straight path,\" one should not fall into the delusion that every desire is rightful and true, but remember that the responsibility lies with themselves. In conclusion, without Allah's will, neither the servant's will nor their purpose is realized; therefore, humility and reliance upon Allah (tawakkul) are essential."